---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'CATEGORY', title: 'The right primary category', text: 'The first field that tells Google what you do. We look at your competitors’ categories and set yours accordingly.' },
  { label: 'SERVICES', title: 'Service list', text: 'We list every service you offer under the name customers search for. We use the same names as the service pages on your site.' },
  { label: 'REVIEWS', title: 'Review flow', text: 'We set up a routine for asking for reviews: regularly, one customer at a time, no incentives, no picking. We reply to every review that comes in.' },
  { label: 'POSTS', title: 'Posts and photos', text: 'New services, offers, the team and the premises. We keep the profile alive with recent posts and real photos.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'nedir', label: 'What profile management means' },
  { id: 'kategori', label: 'Which category?' },
  { id: 'hizmetler', label: 'Services and description' },
  { id: 'yorumlar', label: 'Reviews' },
  { id: 'gonderi', label: 'Posts and photos' },
  { id: 'kurallar', label: 'Shortcuts we don’t take' },
  { id: 'surec', label: 'How we work' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is profile management a one-off job?',
    a: 'No. The category and service structure is set up correctly once. If the flow of reviews, posts and photos stops, the profile falls behind your competitors, so the work continues.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How frequently should I change my category?',
    a: 'Once you’ve found the right category, don’t change it unless you have to. After every change the ranking takes time to settle, and changes made as experiments make it harder to see what’s working.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you get a fake negative review removed?',
    a: 'If it breaks the rules, we report it to Google, but Google decides whether it comes down and the process can take weeks. The lasting fix is to grow the number of real reviews and lower the weight of any single one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to give you access to my profile?',
    a: 'Yes, you add us as a manager. You stay the primary owner of the profile; we don’t ask you to transfer ownership. When you stop working with us, you remove our access.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services/gbp"
  title="Google Business Profile Management Service | Wiserrise"
  description="Category, services, reviews, posts and photos. We manage your Google Business Profile within the rules and report the work we do every week."
  eyebrow="SERVICE · BUSINESS PROFILE"
  h1="We manage your Google Business Profile within the rules."
  crumb="Google Business Profile"
  lede="Google Business Profile management means setting up the profile that represents you on the map correctly and keeping it alive: category, services, reviews, posts and photos. More than anything else, this profile decides your place on the map. We do this work within Google’s rules and don’t take shortcuts that bend them."
  serviceName="Google Business Profile Management"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: p('services') },
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'We fix your profile and your site together so nearby customers can see you.' },
    { label: 'Map Rank Tracking', href: p('services/rank-tracking'), text: 'We measure where you rank from different points around you.' },
    { label: 'Content Production', href: p('services/content'), text: 'For every service on your profile, we write the matching page on your site.' },
    { label: 'GEO', href: p('services/geo'), text: 'We work on getting you mentioned in ChatGPT, Gemini and Google’s AI answers.' },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="nedir">What does Google Business Profile management mean?</h2>

  <p>
    Setting up the profile that represents you on the map correctly and keeping it alive. When someone in Kadıköy searches
    for “dentist”, Google shows three businesses on the map; each of those three cards is a Business Profile.
  </p>

  <p>
    Google decides the order on the map by looking at three things: whether you do the work being searched for, how close
    you are to the searcher and how well known you are. You can’t change proximity. Google reads most of the other two from
    your profile.
  </p>

  <p>
    We see many businesses that set up their profile once and leave it. Setup happens once, and if reviews, posts and
    photos stop coming in, the profile stands still while competitors move ahead.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>If you’d like to check it yourself first:</strong> our <a href={p('tools/gbp-audit')}>free profile audit
      tool</a> shows the weak spots in your profile in 10 questions. It asks for no sign-up or email, and the calculation
      runs in your browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="kategori">Which category should you choose?</h2>

  <p>
    The one that describes your work most narrowly and most accurately. The primary category is the strongest field telling
    Google what you do. Of all the fields you can change on the profile, it’s also the one with the biggest effect on
    ranking.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Turkish category list has many names that sit very close to each other, and businesses frequently pick a
    neighboring, wrong category. Say you’re a lawyer who also works as a mediator. If you choose “Mediator” as your primary
    category, you drop in searches for “lawyer”; the right move is to make “Lawyer” primary and add mediation as an
    additional category.
  </p>

  <p>
    With additional categories, “the more the better” doesn’t work. Google itself asks you to choose the fewest categories
    that describe your business. Every unrelated additional category blurs which searches Google should put you in.
  </p>

  <p>What we do in the category work:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>We list the primary and additional categories of the competitors in the top three</li>
    <li>We compare the categories against your target searches</li>
    <li>We fix the primary category to the main service that brings in the most work</li>
    <li>We keep additional categories limited to services you really offer</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="hizmetler">What do we write in the service list and description?</h2>

  <p>
    Every service you offer, under the name customers search for and with a short description. On most profiles this field
    is either empty or filled with the generic names Google suggests, which makes it identical to your competitors’.
  </p>

  <p>
    We keep service names the same as the pages on your site. If the profile says “Physiotherapy for knee pain”, the site
    has a page with that name too, and Google sees the same business and the same service in two places. If the page
    doesn’t exist, we write it as part of <a href={p('services/content')}>content production</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the business description we explain in plain language what you do and where you are. Google doesn’t want links or
    discount announcements in this field, and we don’t stuff keywords into it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Opening hours and attributes are part of this work too. Showing as open on your profile while you’re closed for a
    public holiday turns customers away at the door and brings a bad review right after.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yorumlar">How do we collect and reply to reviews?</h2>

  <p>
    We set up a routine for asking for reviews: regularly, one customer at a time (no bulk sends), with no incentives like
    gifts or discounts. Picking only the customers you think are satisfied breaks Google’s rules, so we don’t pick. We
    reply to every review that comes in. The content of a review matters as much as the count and the rating: the service and district a customer mentions
    are an extra signal for Google.
  </p>

  <p>
    That’s why we don’t send “give us 5 stars” messages. We give the customer a short link or a QR code and ask them to
    describe the service they received in their own words. We don’t hand out ready-made text; reviews where everyone pastes
    the same sentence look fake to readers and get caught in Google’s filter.
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>A review request that goes out right after the job is done</li>
    <li>One-step reviews through a short link and a QR code on the counter</li>
    <li>A natural reply to every review that mentions the service received</li>
    <li>With a negative review, talking to the customer first, then writing a calm public reply</li>
    <li>Reporting fake reviews that contain insults or clearly come from a competitor to Google</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Some of the reviews we report stay up; Google has the final say. What reduces the effect of a bad review most is the
    real reviews that come after it. We explain the steps for collecting reviews in our
    <a href={p('guides/more-reviews')}>review guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gonderi">Do posts and photos work?</h2>

  <p>
    They do, though they won’t lift you to the top on their own. Google tells a maintained profile from an abandoned one, and
    regular posts and new photos are the most visible sign of that. With everything else equal, a live profile gets ahead of
    a dormant one.
  </p>

  <p>
    In posts we share a new service, an offer, an event or the answer to a common question. We link every post to the
    matching page on the site and add a UTM tag to the link. That way we see visits from the profile separately on the site.
  </p>

  <p>
    We have one rule for photos: they must be real. Exterior, entrance, interior, team and the work itself. We don’t use
    stock images, because when a customer arrives at your door they should find the place they saw on the profile.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kurallar">Which shortcuts don’t we take?</h2>

  <p>
    We don’t do anything that breaks Google’s rules, even if the client asks. These tricks seem to work for a while, then
    the profile gets suspended and all the effort is gone in a day.
  </p>

  <h3>Adding keywords to the business name</h3>

  <p>
    The name on the profile must match the name on your sign. Writing “Mavi Çınar Fizyoterapi Kadıköy Herniated Disc
    Treatment” instead of “Mavi Çınar Fizyoterapi” breaks Google’s rules. We know this can lift rankings in the short term,
    and we still don’t do it.
  </p>

  <p>
    If a competitor does this, you can report it to Google with the “Suggest an edit” option in Maps. Once the name follows
    the rules, that profile loses the boost it got from the keyword.
  </p>

  <h3>Fake or bought reviews</h3>

  <p>
    Asking for reviews in exchange for money, discounts or gifts, having employees and acquaintances write reviews, buying
    reviews: all of it breaks Google’s rules. Asking only the customers you think are satisfied is also against the rules. We
    don’t pick who to ask, and we reply to the bad reviews too.
  </p>

  <h3>Unnecessary extra profiles</h3>

  <p>
    One profile is opened for each real branch. If you have no branches, opening a separate profile for each district or
    each service breaks the rules. A virtual office address where no one is present during opening hours doesn’t count as
    valid for a profile either.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you work without showing an address, we set up the profile with a service area. There’s a misconception here: adding
    20 districts to your service area doesn’t mean you’ll show up on the map in those districts. Google still calculates
    the ranking by proximity to where your business is located.
  </p>

  <p>
    The way to show up in surrounding districts runs through your site, and we build that in the
    <a href={p('services/local-seo')}>local SEO</a> work. If you don’t have a profile yet, we explain the steps in the
    <a href={p('guides/create-profile')}>profile setup guide</a>, and if you’re stuck on verification, in the
    <a href={p('guides/verification')}>verification guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="surec">How do we work?</h2>

  <p>We measure first, then touch the profile.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Audit.</strong> We map out your profile as it stands today, the category and service structure of the
      competitors in the top three and the review gap between you. We take <a href={p('services/rank-tracking')}>measurement
      across your area</a> and see where you rank from different points around you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Setup.</strong> Category, service list, description, hours, attributes and photos get fixed. Most of the work
      comes out at this stage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Flow.</strong> The post calendar and the review request flow start, and every incoming review gets a reply.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measurement.</strong> We read how your ranking changes together with profile interactions, meaning the
      number of calls, direction requests and messages.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    We can’t give you a timeframe; it depends on where your business starts, on the competition and on the channel. What we
    can tell you is the order of the work: we measure first, fix the root problems, then produce. You see what we did and
    how the measurement changed in your weekly report.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Where is your profile <span class="highlight">weak</span>?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
